    "summary": "Acoustic gems, rare Pigpen organ, solid blues set with stellar guitar",
    "blurb": "The show delivers an unforgettable acoustic first half filled with rare tracks like \"Katie Mae\" and a bluegrass‑flavored \"Little Sadie.\" After Phil's amp blew the band pivots to a blues‑heavy second set highlighted by Pigpen on organ. Soundboard clarity is exceptional, making this a must‑listen early‑Dead performance.",
    "review": "January 31, 1970 at The Warehouse in New Orleans captured a quirky snapshot of the Grateful Dead before they fully embraced the jam‑centric era. An unexpected bass amp failure forced Phil Lesh off his instrument midway through the first set, prompting the band to launch into an acoustic mini‑set that has become legendary among collectors – Jerry’s bluegrass finesse shines on \"Little Sadie\" and a heartfelt solo on \"Katie Mae,\" while Pigpen adds depth on organ throughout. The second set recovers with a blues‑laden groove; standout moments include a gritty rendition of \"Hard To Handle,\" country‑tinged vocals from Bob Weir on \"Long Black Limousine\" and \"Seasons Of My Heart,\" and the rare inclusion of \"Saw Mill\" and \"The Race Is On.\" With crystal‑clear soundboard capture, the performance feels both intimate and vibrant, offering a rare glimpse of early Dead chemistry and improvisational spirit.",
